Dilace to the death: MP3 players against cell phones
Adheses how to choose
While almost every phone can read the MP3 files, no MP3 player can call.
From this it is clear that it’s better to just buy the phone.
Despite this, there are many reasons to obtain an autonomous reader.
Deciation depends entirely on the mode of use and the needs of the future owner.
Maybe you'd hardly buy yourself an expensive high-of-the-in-chid to listen to Maxim Turbulenc's latest album.
First of all these musical products do not go out as LP and from a qualitative point of view for this there is not even an objective reason.
But if on your sheras are crowded hard-rock,jazz or blues vinyls probably, a turntable of its kind at a minimum you are dreaming about it.
It also works with portable music, i.e. compressed music or simply in MP3 format.
Even here there are prices and quality categories.
For some it may be unacceptable to spend 8,000 kroner for a reader, as well as a Pink Floyd fan perhaps hardly dismantling headphones from twenty kneales of the market.
There are two extremes among which there is enough room for everyone.
The choice is wide.
Deciding is the durability of the batteries.
Some high-quality music players can do a lot more than simply read the music, but something they don't make cell phones even by mistake.
There isn’t any other current device as universal as your cell phone.
In addition to the primary functions, calling and texting, he can read music and videos, record both and have a camera, sometimes better sometimes worse and maybe he can even navigate.
And if we take into account smartphones and handhelds you will also have a thumbnail computer in your pocket.
“It’s kind of a small miracle that no refrigerator can do the grill and no television can photograph anything.
The undifferentiality of the cell phone also carries disadvantages.
Multiple functions you are using on your mobile phone and the sooner the indicator on the screen will require the battery charger.
“Threw music consumes a lot of energy.
A few cell phones are able to read the music for more than twelve hours, usually, and much less.
In this sense music players (absolutely the simple ones) are much more effective.
As far as inexpensive phones do not count that they can last for more than a few hours.
If you’ll still add to it, from time to time, get another battery charger at work for safety, that’s also true for the most expensive fixtures.
But you won’t have this problem with an MP3 player, even the worst ones last at least 9-hour continuous functionality with a recharging.
An additional problem exists when you download the music in your cell phone.
“Unfortunately, only a limited portion of devices has the universal USB port, and in addition, not all of them can be connected to the computer without special software.
Of course the music can be recorded directly on the memory card, but only a few memory players are able to do it without an adapter for minicards that are not used in mobile phones.
Readers, with a few exceptions (Ipods) do not need any special software.
It is enough to replenish the device with the music via USB from the computer.
The majority of mobile phones and readers create for themselves a multimedia library ordered according to the names of the interpreters, the name of the album, genre or according to the user's classification.
Some devices only re-produce the mast structure of the folders, similar to the Explorer tool in Windows, and some can do both (iriver, Samsung, Sony, Cowon, intelligent phones with an operating system).
Readers are easier to handle, cell phones can do more.
When you choose a particular device you are mainly interested in battery strength, memory capacity, the quality and the type of audio outputs and clearly the ease of use.
If the music you need above all in the background and if you listen to it every day only briefly on the tram you only need any mobile phone that reads MP3.
This now succeeds, of course to any cell phone.
You don’t have to deal too much with what your cell phone or reader formats recognize.
“MP3 players support them all, the global majority of the music that comes to you will be right in this format.
In addition to this format that belongs to the most popular ones, the total majority of luminaires support WMA music.
There’s less the AAAC (formatted used by the company Apple and its Ipods).
The best cellphones also allow, for example, to buy music online.
It is enough to visit, through the mobile network, the correspondent pages of the operator (or another supplier) and buy the song or album.
This same service could already be a valid argument for buying a cell phone-reader.
"Unfortunately the offer of the operators is meager and it is usually limited only to the hit of the moment of the radio.
Pricing isn’t really low.
But it is generally presuppried that precisely this mode of selling music (whether via GSM , internet or wireless internet) in the future will dominate the music industry in the future.
Most people always carry their cell phones with them.
To anyone who wants to hear something fruguous in his pocket while the MP3 player remains forgotten at home.
However, it also depends on this, if you always carry the headphones behind or at at least the cordless headset.
If you don’t, you’ll be able to use the built-initiated player that almost every mobile phone, but only a small portion of MP3 players has.
Perhaps the biggest advantage of cellphone-to-readers will be, for many, the price.
The cheaper ones normally cost less than 3 thousand crowns.
For 4,000 kroner, you’ll already buy a dignified walkman.
You’ll usually be limited by the absence of an input for common headphones.
Prompt mobile phones with the most well-furnied and technical-propelled cellphones with 3.5 mm for common headphones cost up to 15,000 kroner.
On the other hand, the advantage of the readers is, in addition to others, the easy enlivolcity.
The simple ones that are not necessarily cheaper (see our choice) makes it really anyone to handle them.
The slightest of the functions, the similarity of manevability at one time with the common stereo or perhaps the turn-bearer, is obvious.
There is no danger of mistakenly hitting the launch of the mobile internet network and as a consequence of a giant bill for the transmission of data.
Which device to take
Even the most economical mobile phones (e.g. Vodafone 527) usually have an interchangeable memory-poc card slot (of M2, microSD, etc.).
Also with regard to the purchase of the respective card, fall within 2 000 kneales.
But be careful
The base models only support cards with a maximum capacity of 2GB.
It’s only the more expensive ones that can also be done with 8GB memory cards.
If you’ve already got a mobile and you want to buy an inexpensive MP3 player, take Cowon, Creative, Sony, Samsung, Emgeton or iriver.
According to capacity (max. 2 GB) you don’t have to spend more than 1,100 kroner.
If you’re part of the most discerning listeners, preferably take specialized cell phones – walkman (Sony Ericsson, some Nokia and Samsung).
These luminaires have individual MP3 streak buttons and the convenience of the service approaches the same MP3 player.
In this context, Sony Ericsson has made its way the most, whose walk-in line mobile phones have the same fluency as MP3-beams sold separately.
The MP3 players of the brands indicated above ensure the same service and price category up to 4,000 kroner, but with a larger memory capacity (up to 16 GB) and battery resistance (commonly 15 hours, in principle for up to 35 hours).
The first virtual operator will reach the Czech Republic as early as this year.
- Maybe it's
Possibly the mobile telephony market in the Czech Republic is quite mature and our operators offer practically all the services like their partners in Western Europe.
However, there’s something’s missing—virtual operator that obliges traditional ones to lower the price.
Maybe he won't be missing for a long time.
Of the fact that the Czech operators belong to the most cherished in Europe we have already written many times on Mobil.cz.
At the end of the day, “T” also confirmed it, which pointed out that the price of text messages is higher by us than in the rest of the Union.
After the last steps of the European Commission in the field of regularization of roaming tariffs it will perhaps create a paradoxical situation - for some customers of Czech mobile operators the SMS will cost them less from abroad than at home.
“How is it possible that prices in the Czech Republic remain so high towards Europe?
“The answer’s simple, it lacks the competition that would really like to start the fight.
Possibly it sounds absurd, even here we have three movable nets and coverage highly over 100 %.
Mobile TLophony operators like to complain that the Czech market is extremely competitive.
In this sense it often also emphasizes the head of the Czech Telecommunications Council, Pavel Dvo?k , who in the past has sometimes expressed that there is no place for another operator in the Czech Republic.
“Perhaps it’s true that the traditional operator with their own network doesn’t probably have a lot of chance anymore. (The rest maybe it’d be difficult to find other frequencies now).
There's still another possibility - the virtual operator.
Precisely the entry into the market of virtual operators has led to a considerable decrease in prices, to which the traditional operators would then have to react.
Simultaneously even before the entry of sharks such as Tesko Mobile and Virgin Mobile on the market, similar voices sounded in the United Kingdom.
It has been shown, however, that the place for such operators exists and that they can be really successful.
At the end of the day we don’t have to go very far.
In February of this year we have informed you of the arrival of virtual operator Mobilking in Poland.
In only half a year this operator has managed to buy more than 100 thousand customers and become the fifth-largest local operator.
At the same time behind Mobilking lies the Penta financial group to which in the Czech Republic among other things U:fon.
“Virtual operators’ advantage is the ability to focus on a much more concrete target-group.
To be able to actually work they need many fewer customers than to a traditional operator.
They don’t carry around, i.e., relatively high expenses for the creation, operation, and maintenance of the mobile network.
So in the world virtual operators usually come at a low cost, they offer low-riginal calls and SMS, which is often in balance with the offer of the most restricted services.
So for example Tesco Mobile in the United Kingdom offers SMS that cost at a minimum half compared to its competitors.
But just as Mobilking’s example in Poland shows it can also be a pretty good bet on a not-small-chapion-group.
This operator focuses specifically on humans.
“True men choose Mobilking operator that they like and can be buophasized,” says David Seibertz, CEO Mobile Entertainment Company.
Difficulcult to say about whether a similar concept can also be affirmed by us as in Poland.
Vodafone spokesman Miroslav Epick: "The Czech market is relatively small for a fragmented approach, on which the majority of virtual operators are based.
In addition, in terms of the services it is relatively saturated."
In the Czech Republic there is no possibility, say the operators of the mobile network
But it seems almost certain that if there will ever be an operator in the Czech Republic it will not be Mobilking.
"There is talk of the expansion of this successful project, but in the Czech Republic negotiations do not have a lot of expectations.
The ignition of Mobilking will happen rather in the other countries of our area," he said in a telephone dialogue for Mobile.cz Jana Studni?kov?, the press spokesman of the Penta financial group.
So he indirectly confirmed that the current operators in the Czech Republic are definitely not very in favor of this thought.
On the contrary, they try to do everything because you don’t create a potential competitor.
For some time it does not hide its own interest in starting a virtual operator, the GTS Novera group that would thus like to take another step to become a real competitor of Telefanika O2 in the field of corporate customers.
"We have a great deal of interest in being able to offer our customers not only fixed services but mobile telecommunications.
And the sooner it's done the better.
“We’ve repeatedly negotiated on this possibility with all Czech mobile network operators.
"We've already managed to make progress in negotiations with some of them," Group spokesman Pavel Kaidl said.
So maybe better times will come.
Uncommissioned information it emerges that the first virtual operator will perhaps have to have it by the end of the year.
But for now around his creation stands an impenetrable wall of silence.
“Right now we don’t believe that in the Czech market there’s space for creating a full-fledged virtual operator.
The Czech market, unlike other markets, is very specific.
“Until now, no subject with a complex and serious project has addressed us,” he mentioned in his statement for Mobil.cz Vlastimil Sr?e of Telefònika O2.
To the specificity of the Czech market also the other operators recur.
T-Mobil press spokesman Martina Kemrov clatter adds in a rare agreement with the O2 statement: "For now we have not noticed any viable and advantageous commercial project for both of them, on which it is possible to continue building".
“In T-Mobil, a virtual operator is unlikely to appear by the end-of-the-nd-down-the-year.
“Right at the moment we have already had to have a concrete agreement on the table so that it would be possible to consider it on the horizon.”
Vodafone concentrates its attention rather than achieving its 3G license requests.
Miroslav adds: "We are open towards the negotiations of the potential virtual operators and this especially in the context of the sharing of expenses and infrastructure.
Let’s consider this, from a customer’s point of view, the most producing.
Vodafone's main strategy in this area is to find a partner to build a common network for fast-paced mobile internet."
Compreciating authorities -with complicit operators
The attitude of mobile operators can be easily understood, the entry of another operator would mean an increase in the competition, it would lead to the lowering of prices and with this to the decrease in profit.
So why facilitate the rise of such a contestant?
Inconveniences around creation U:fon testify that when the operators hold to one thing they can row on the same side.
Possibly the great pressure pressed against "T" during the creation period of U:fon towards the T-Mobile company.
This is a testament to public access-by-in-the-line web-pages.
But right now U:fon serves as an argument for which you don't accept the virtual operator.
Martina Kemrov said: “The environment of competition in the mobile telephony market has been poorly empowered beyond all with the entry of the fourth mobile operator into the market.
With regard to the typology of the movable market in the Czech Republic it is obvious that the entry of another contestant would place on him and above all on his pretensivious services."
Of the attitude of the mobile operators, however, the complete displeasurement on the part of the Czech regulatory bodies.
It’s not just a matter of “TAT” attitude that unfortunately in Czech Reppublica—unlike Britain and other Developed Countries—has no proficiency at interfering in economic competition.
It belongs exclusively to the Economic Competition Protection Office (OHS).
This, however, with regard to the whole problem has taken its typical position.
“Virtual operator’s entry into the mobile telephony market would be possible after the conduiture of the leased arrangement of the necessary infrastructure between a mobile operator and a virtual operator had been contruded.
“No such contract has been concluded so far because the offer of potential buyers probably wasn’t attractive enough for traditional traders,” said Kristi?n Chalupa, of the OHS External Relations section in responding to our question.
Such a formulation statement by the representatives of the operators perhaps does not surprise.
On the other hand, on the part of the office that has to defend the interests of consumers and moreover take care of the fact that no one takes advantage of his position on the market, it is more than a delicate affair.
Particularly when Kristion Chalupa adds: “Abroad where virtual operators commonly work, we can observe that their entry into the market has contributed to the lowering of prices for mobile telephony services.”
But of course the pressure on the proper functioning of the market environment and the lowering of prices do not belong to OHS interests.
Truly chocking in the whole situation is the almost thrilling harmony of these three operators resulting from their reactions.
The question as to whether it's really just a coincidence, arises on its own.
Equally as the exact price of gasoline for all gasoline gasoline workers is certainly the result of coincidence and competitive strife.
"The section found no signs of anti-competitive behavior regarding this fact and therefore has no reason to intervene in any way.
No operator has a dominance, so it does not have a misrehesponslerd greater responsibility for its behaviour on the market.
Necessary tools for the opening of the mobile network market for virtual operators are perhaps also lacking the te-Telecommunications Regecutor," Chalupa mentioned in his statement.
Activating the dominance on the market is one of many controversial points.
Of course there is an analysis of the relevant markets drawn up by ?T, according to which te telecommunications is the only market from the point of view of economic competition.
On such a market, of course, no Czech operator has a dominant position.
But some juridical savves offer another approach - for the creation of a virtual operator there is always the agreement of the operator of the network.
And the movable-operator, at the out of all, logically – the monopoly of his network.
So for a certain portion it must necessarily be dominant.
But even if we didn’t admit this argument, OHS should be a modifier that three movable railers perform so strangely similar.
At the same time the complaints from the interested parties (included GTS Novera) show that during the negotiations with all the operators they encounter very similar obstacles.
Discharging accountability in telecommunications in telecommunications isn’t a fairly frequent trick with which OHS keeps you out of the whole thing.
We’ve already seen this in some similar cases.
“Unfortunately it has not been possible to promote the expansion of “T” rights to economic competition in telecommunications in telecommunications until the recently updated telecommunications law, despite the fact that many experts were expected to improve the situation.
OHS’s representatives need to know this more than well, but perhaps their own interests are elsewhere.
It can be done when no one looks
The functioning of such pseudo-virtual operators remains, of which there are even some in the Czech mobile networks.
These are companies that publish their price lists of mobile telephony services but are only partially public.
It’s usually multi-level marketing companies.
For example Amway, Bohemia Telecom, Starlife and a number of others offer their own mobile phone services.
The price lists of these companies usually only reach predetermined people.
“Magic lies in the fact that the company vis-à-vis the operator appears as a classic business customer, ordering a large amount of SIM cards.
As an important customer then receives offers of special prices (and more).
The problem is that the commercial conditions of all the Czech mobile telecom operators prohibit a similar presale.
"Should the cases you are talking about, of which we will be interested, it could be a violation of the contractual conditions, and it could lead to the conclusion of the cooperation with that company," Martina Kemrov? replied.
It’s quite improbable that such an activity didn’t come to the ears of those responsible for mobile operators.
Particularly in the cases of the 3 companies mentioned there can be a number of thousands of customers.
No operator defends himself at the same time from such an attitude.
From the activity of these companies they derive a profit not indifferent and at the same time - unlike the real virtual operators - they are not harmed in any way with their official marketing.
Miroslav “epick” warns about possible problems: “The law allows similar speculation, but only a neglicessable percentage of customers use the services of these companies.
They also have to accept that the SIM card in their phone is not registered in their name, therefore they cannot fully dispose of it as the regular customers of mobile operators.”
However, this should be the slightest strange at the “T”.
There’s also another issue for OHS.
If movable operators close their eyes in the face of such an attitude, why do they block the creation of the virtual operator?
It is necessary to add that “OHS has strong enough rights to unmask similar activities and if you don’t want, you won’t have to be satisfied with the explanation of the operators who have no idea of everything and that their commercial conditions foretell.
When we won't be more expensive
As has already been said in the text several times, the entry of virtual operators into the market of mobile telephony has brought a considerable fall in prices for services.
At the same time in almost all the countries where operators were born, traditional operators tried more or less to impede their creation.
Arguments that they used were virtually the same—coasurmount market too small, too sizor-diginning, problems with the quality of services etc.
But in the developed countries precisely in these cases the regulatory bodies have intervened in defense not of mobile telephony operators but of their customers.
Both with the formal measures and with non-formle formal pressure, then we arrived at the creation of the first virtual operator and it was joined by others.
“Unfortunately, the Czech regulatory bodies launch from one to the other the case as a hot potato.
For our experience we cannot get rid of the feeling that non-clealarity of skills in these cases makes him more than comfortable.
Don't throw the sponge.
If the backsto-scenario information is right and the virtual operator will really appear soon with us, the Czech mobile phone market will get back in motion.
“Whether one of the operators has consented to the creation of a virtual-movable operator, OHS could be impassively unaffected by the others concerned.
And maybe we're going to quit calling at higher prices than the rest of Europe.
The new anthem has four new versions.
Choose the Best One
Hear the four new versions of the Czech national anthem.
Prime Minister Mirek Topol?nek on the occasion of the Czech State Recognition on Sunday presented in a solemn way the new recordings of the national anthem "Kde domov mij".
