In A Separate City, Pride and Trials of a Dark Life
Tucked away in the extended theme park on the suburbs of Orlando, just a few miles of splendour, noise, the huge, lies the quiet city where pride and complications of the African-espi-Ospino-Engho-experience come into life.
Eatonville (Eakeville), the first all-black city embedded in the country and Zora Neale Hurston’s birthplace, is no longer as simple as it described it in 1935: “the city of five lakes, three croquet fields, 300 brown skins, 300 good swimmers, an abundamy guava, two schools and no prisons.”
It’s now a place of pilgrimage.
“ Alice Walker, Toni Morrison and Ruby Dee have come to the year-old Zora! Festival at Eatonville to show their respect to Hurston, Harlem Renaissance’s most famous writer.
Yet in a number of ways in a number of ways, the city it has described – and which makes tourists stop by include the Florida tour guide brewed by the Depression—remains a place apart.
“It’s as independent, dignified, and private as it was in the 1930s, when Hurston wrote that rural blacks in Florida often resisted to share their thoughts with the white man that “knowing so little about us, he doesn’t know what he’s missing.”
Even now, in a year in a year when a black presidential nominee, Senator Barack Obama, was called for an opening speech on the race, many here remain displeatable of the outsider’s gaze.
“We’re very cautious about how our story is being told,” said Hortense Jones, 59—a life-manufacturer and a member of the city’s oldest church.
"It has to be right."
Eatonville has long been defined as a paradox of triumph and hardship.
“It’s both a historic black take-up model and a nearly 2,400-nation community where the poverty rate is twice the national average.
It is a literary center but also an example in the shadow of the oaks of the rural southern black culture - sometimes disdained, sometimes rewarded - born from American slavery.
Unsurpriprisingly, residents here are both proud and protective.
And attention to Eatonville's imagination really began with Zora, which everyone here calls Hurston.
She presented the world to her hometown through sincere, dialect-rich books such as "Mules and Men" (1935) and "Their Eyes Were Watching God" (1937).
Five-paragraphs of the Florida guide transformed the city, just outside Rout 17, a road that runs through the often-forgotten center-to-trees of Florida into a scenario of black history and human drama.
A bas-skewer in both life and literature, Hurston took readers to the store possessed by Eatonville’s first mayor, Joe Clarke, before diversing in more private areas.
"Get out the street on the left," he wrote, "it's the modern public school, brown with white trimmings, with its well-kept gardens and courtyards, to which Howard Miller always looked, thinking he could barely read and write."
She also mentioned the widow's new-husband Dash and wrote that Lee Glenn "swrenders beverages of all kinds and whatever fits the passing rooms".
In just a few hundred words, Hurston connected Eatonville with self-governation but also illiteracy, second-marriage and sex.
Clearly this wasn't Fodor.
It wasn’t a portrait that everyone appreciated.
"Zora used to say it the way it was," said Ella Dinkins, a 90-year-old, one of Johnson's girls whom Hurston immortalizes circhanting coarse chanted songs about their beauty.
She adds, "Some people don't like it."
“Hurston is still remembered here as an ectizophal lively who frequently returned from her family who moved to Jacksonville, Fla.
Augustus Franklin, 77 years old, remembers that when Hurston snumbled into the city, he habitually arrived unannounced at a triumphant Chevrolt, smoking and wearing trousers in a city that even today boasts dignificent attire.
“Damnumbered people were fascinated,” says Mr. Franklin, while others sneered.
“The people were always very pleased to see Zora,” Mr. Franklin said.
But, he added, rocking on his chair on the back patio overlooking Lake Sabelia, where Hurston was most likely christened, "she never stayed for long."
“When Hurston died in the 1960s, he was poor and his books had gone in print.
Together with the rest of the world, Eatonville seemed to have forgotten it.
Although she was once a star of the literature, a Langston Hughes-in-divisor and the only black woman at Barnard College in the 1920s, she was groribbed in an unlatted péceon at Fort Pierce, Fla, where she had lived.
“At Eatonville, there weren’t great commemorative ceremonies, and no major conference.
"I don't think they understand her contribution to the world or her bequest to everyone," said Valerie Boyd, "Wrapped in Rainbows," Hurston's biography published in 2003.
A change took place in the 1980s.
Orange County officials wanted to place a five-latin highway across the city to replace Kennedy Boulevard, the two-lane main street.
The expansion of Orlando has already propelled La Interstate 4 via the city's western limit.
The proposal came when Eatonville was still reburiding from a difficult period of its history.
Consecutive integration, in addition to other things, had given an end to its idyllic isolation of the community
In the fiftities, the battle over racial mixing brought hatred to the community’s doorstep.
"During that period, a group of white boys came and threw oranges and things to the people who sat on one side," says Mr. Franklin.
“In fact, we had a girl who was killed by that one time.
Thrished a watermelon out of a car."
“Whether in a 1955 letter to The Orlando Sentinel, Hurston asked the Supreme Court a request for forced integration, calling his decision in Brown against the school office “an insult rather than an honor to my race.”
Responsions now say the desecrimination of schools, despite being positive in some respects, dilutes Eatonville’s cohesion and jeopardises the confidence of its young people.
“Black children were used to being embraced — I remember this — hugging your teacher in the morning, hugging your teacher in the evening,” said N. Y. Nathiri, Ella Dinkins’s daughter, Preserve the Eatonville Community, a non-for-Professor.
It lasted, he added, as long as teachers and the students did not come from the same place.
“You didn’t embrace your white teacher because your white teacher—I mean, there’s a cultural divide there,” Ms. Nathiri said.
The civil rights, in any case, aided and created space for many other Zora Neale Hurstons - black writer, actress and artist who overcame the prejudices, as she did, with meager self-confidence and lines like: "How can some deny themselves the pleasure of my company!
“I just don’t understand.”
In 1975, the writer Alice Walker began a long journey to Hurston’s unlatted tomb and began fighting for the resurrection of her reputation.
Five years later, an augmented Hurston biscuit by Robert E. Hemenway hit bookstore shelves, reintroducing it into the American canon.
The project of the highway arrived just as his most famous daughter had again drawn the limelight.
And this time, Hurston's old neighbors saw it as a savior.
The community began designing a Hurston-dedicated festival in 1988 to show what could ruin the state with its highway.
Thousands of fans came to the inaugural event two years later, and every January, many return for the commemoration.
Several years later, the state backed down from his paving project.
"The resurrected five lanes of the resurrected highway has brought what you would say high-strength, a real pride for the city," Ms. Nathiri said.
Mrs. Boyd puts it in a simpler way: "Zora saved Eatonville."
Victory over the highway project has helped to change the imagining that the city has of itself.
Those from outside the city such as Rachelle Munson, a lawyer who began coming to the church here in 1993, began to appear in large numbers, and the residents began re-erieving the past.
Eatonville was added to the Public Historical Register in 1998.
A one-storey one-story bookstore (call Hurston, of course) opened in 2006 on a replenished and beautified Kennedy Boulevard.
Today, Eatonville remains an anomaly of Florida: only six miles from downtown Orlando, it can give the feeling of a secondary road in the summer rain, small as when it was founded with just 27 black families in the 1880s.
(It’s 90 per cent black today.)
The outsiders who come to look for Eatonville’s story, because of its meaning, are often still treated with caution.
Petition requested in advance is required for the majority of interviews, some things – such as murals on Eatonville’s oldest church, painted by a white man, who show black men in the fields – are not allowed to be photographed.
Lots in Eatonville, such as Ms. Jones, a plump teacher with a penchant for bright red, still fears their insular community would be misunderstood.
Yet, as soon as the Haurston festival has expanded, a high level of city pride has also emerged.
The juveniles, in particular, tend to see Eatonville with Hurston saw his entire race: beautiful, problems and everything, neither better, nor worse and proud, creative, great worker, silly and confused like other races and ethnic groups in America.
There is sincere civic strife what can be heard in Mr. Franklin’s nephew, Edwin Harvey, who plans to go back to Eatonville after college to work in the local government or the Police Department, which he said may need help.
“Those who are younger, such as Alondra and Alexia Kenon, an 11-year-year-year-year-old clumps from Winter Park, also seem learned to describe Eatonville properly.
"A lot of people, if they've just walked in here, they'll think, 'Oh, this city is nothing compared to everyone else,'" Alondra Kenon said after Mass on a recent Sunday.
“But if you really stop and take a moment to look at history, it’s really a pretty city.”
