With a passion for "Cante de las Minas", Bauer in 1997 began a long journey aimed at retracting singers, guitarists or dancers using a slab-to-slab camera.
They are portraits that, due to the type of machine and the material, are reminiscent of classic photography.
The application of different stylistic techniques breaks with the traditional principles of the image, conferring on the photo modernity, according to an idea that reflects Bauer's conception of flamenco: "A music with very traditional roots that evolves and is able to amaze every day."
It’s not the dancing, the singing or playing in itself, but the personality of the artist, his character, capable of conquering the audience with his art.
The search for an encounter with the essential elements, with the magic that becomes visible in a certain way of feeling and of being, are and have been the constant of this work, on display in these days at the El Fotamata gallery.
Famous flamenco artists such as Diego El Cigala, Chano Lobato, Cristina Hoyos
The project has become a sort of an anthology of flamenco, and it will soon be published in a book that will collect the portraits on display on the occasion of the exhibition.
The passion for portraits led Bauer to repeat the idea two years ago with "Portraits of Veteran Athletes" ("SportARTproject").
Although in "Flamenco-People" the concept is different, the photographer admits "I love retracting people, getting to know them and being able to penetrate for a moment in their lives, whether this a long or a short moment.
I think the portrait is the most difficult genre in photography.
You meet or compare with people, characters you sometimes don’t even even know or who only allow you a few minutes to take the picture.
“Beyond these circumstances, such as a Photographer, I always try to realize my ideas.”
"Flamenco-people" is housed at the El Fotamata gallery, at number 20 via de la Mata (via de la Mata, parallel to Via Alameda de Hercules, and will be open to the public until October 16.
Vargas Llosa Reveres the Novel He's Writing Is a "Gambling"
It’s set in places he doesn’t yet know and deals with unfamiliar subjects: despite this, Mario Vargas Llosa is grappling with a new project she’s working on, a novel whose protagonist, Roger Casemant, is an Irish nationalist who has lived twenty years in Congo and has crossed the Amazon.
Always favored for the Nobel Prize in Literature, the writer confessed yesterday on the occasion of the Hay Festival of Segovia, that the project he is dealing with today "is a gamble" and that "everything indicates that I shouldn't write it."
"If I had common sense, I wouldn't write it, but I'll do it anyway and I'm hoping to finish it as soon as possible," he concluded his speech at the festival for the exchange of ideas, thoughts and words that for four days has seen a hundred writers, historians, reporters and thousands of people pass through the city.
"La ciudad y los perros" has been, as per prediction, the leading figure at the third edition of the word's festival.
Vargas Llosa, who won the Hay Festival award to be the most-voiced writer by the attendees – an early edition of Dickens’ “Our Mutual Friends” book, filled the seats of the Juan Bravo theater.
Introceived by Félix Romeo, the writer found himself in front of an audience to which time has flown when the Peruvian, with a prodigious memoristic memoristic memorization and with great doses of humour, evoked how the literary vocation was born, derived from the "extraordinary adventure" of the "pleasure to read" and "living beyond what I was living"; as he writes in "disciplinated way" to bead with the "rescuture".
“Spanic-U.S. novelist acknowledged that “there’s no way there’s “no way” to measure and know if books can change people’s lives, “but I refuse to believe that literature is pure entertainment, an ephemeral experience,” Vargas Llosa pointed out, arguing that, on the contrary, “it has “effects” and “makes the most non-conformist people.”
