In the same way he emphasized that the training is regulated by "very strict ethical codes for all public offices."
"Sangre de mayo", the epic gaiter of Garci
Drawing inspiration from Benito Pérez Galdas’ Episodios Nacionales, Jose Luis Garci re-proposes in Sangre de mayo the famous revolt of the Spaniards against the French, in what is the most ambitious film of his filmography, with almost 15 million euros in a budget.
With this film, the director tried to "illustrate the torrent of characters and at the same time the social analysis" he has always admired in the work of the famous novelist, he explained today during the presentation of the film, with Quim Gutierrez and Paula Echevarra at the head of a large cast of interpreters.
Sangre de mayo, more than two-and-a-half-strong hours, debuts this Friday in theaters of the theaters of the cinema, to become in the future a four-hour television miniseries on TeleMadrid.
A great admirer of the literature of Galdas, Jose Luis Garci claims to have realized "a dream of his childhood", and believes he must thank Esperanza Aguirre, who from the Community of Madrid had commissioned him three years before shooting a commemorative film for the second centenary of May 2 medrilegno. The director had accepted as long as the story based on the novel Episodios Nacionales.
“Purposed €15 million allotted in full by the Community of Madrid for Garci a “unicipal opportunity” to re-encreased sets and customs to portray a stunned Spain that erred the choice of the leader for whom to fight and the fact that the struggle for independence led to the people a huge lag in development under the reign of Ferdinand VII,” Garci noted.
However, the director regrets not having received funding equal to those of Alatriste or Los fantasmas de Goya - more than twice as much as Sangre de mayo, so it was not possible to shoot some scenes planned by the scenography that he himself had signed together with Horacio Valcarcel, a collaborator of almost his entire career.
“This, for Volver a empezar’s director, has influenced what’s his practice of habitual work, staging with the actors, with which he often uses to try before filming with those who will not be part of the cast, because he considers it “humiliating”, considering that “part of the director’s work lies in going to the theater and the cinema to know what’s going on in the industry in which he works.”
Many of the performers, some of whom fall into Sangre de mayo's cast, prove in front of his car, such as Michael Rell, Carlos Larranaga or Fernando Guillen Cuervo.
Oscar nominee four times - winning one - Garci believes that none of the three films nominees to represent Spain this year at the Hollywood Academy - his Sangre de mayo, Siete mesas de billar francés and, the last selected Los girasoles ciegos - do not represent the youngest generation of Spanish cinema "which should instead be known."
Garci doesn't expect awards from this film.
"At the beginning you think you're working for posterity, then you realize you're only working for prosperity, and at some point conform that you're just turning an extra film," Garci explains after more than a 30-year career.
Isabel Bayon Rediscovers Seviglian Essence of Dancer Tortola Valencia
An Sevilian ballerina pays tribute to another ballerina of Sevilla.
Carmen Valencia Tortola (Firenze Tortola Ferrer) and Andalusian mother (Georgina Valencia Valenzuela), was born in the Triana district of Triana, and at the age of three moved with his family to London.
She was a free-dancing dancer and a specialist in oriental dances.
She led a fascinating life and was a pioneer of female emancipation, the caliber of Isadora Duncan, Virginia Woolf or Sarah Bernhardt.
“He was a buddhist and atromancy, and he defended the cause for the abolition of the corset that prevented women from moving freely.
The life of this woman, who marked a revolution in the field of dance and aesthetics in Europe between the two wars, has always fascinated Isabel Bayon.
"I'd have loved working with Pepa Gamboa and she offered me this character. It seemed unbelievable."
The ballerina argues: "we do not pretend to tell his whole life, but to collect his spirit.
The titles of his dances have suggested to me some basic elements of flamenco.
Thus, "dancing de la mariposa" suggested a guajira, or "dancing de la serpiente" some thongs.
“His dance was sensual and I’ve been told that even my way of dancing fits into this aesthetic,” he explains.
The staging includes two exceptional contributions: that of Miguel Poveda, who will sang Isabel again in one of her shows, such as "La puerta abierta"; and that of the teacher Matilde Coral, who exceptionally wanted to work with what had been her student.
"Matilde plays Tortola as an adult, the character of maturity.
“I’m happy because he has agreed to be working in my work and I feel very honored.”
The staging also includes the musical composition and guitarists such as Jesus Torres and Paco Arriaga, Miguel Ortega's song, the Pulga y Moi of Moron; the percussions of El P?jaro, as well as Rafa La Viola's viola and the collaboration of an actor, Juanfran Ju?rez "who interprets the role of eternal accompanist of these artists who have always been well-rounded dive, and who have always had a lot."
In the work there is in some form also the life of this woman that takes place in distant places, in particular for that time, such as India, the United States or Russia, and then the whole of Europe, since, habitually, she resided in London.
"I wanted to remember her, because I think she's one of the women who have had a huge influence in a given historical period and now no one reminds herself and so much less considers her work as something of valor."
The show will begin today at 8.30 p.m. at Lope de Vega.
The "Diario de un poeta recién casado" the Triennium also closes
The presentation of the first illustrated edition of "Diario de un Poeta Reciencasado" at the church of Saint Stephen (New York), where Juan Ramon Jiménez married Zenobia in March 1916, is part of the activities planned by the Department of the Culture of the Junta of Andalusia to celebrate the "Triennium of Zenobia and Juan Ramon" (2006-2008), organized to commemorate the fiftieth anniversary of the Nobel Prize.
Thus, the "Triennium of Zenobia and Juan Ramon" ends with the re-edition of this work, with the International Congress that took place in New York, with the restoration of the house-wamuseum of the Nobel and with the traveling exhibition "Juan Ram?n Jiménez: Aque chopo de luz", which was inaugurated last May and which, after touching the various provinces of Andalusia.
In addition, the Department of Culture of the Junta of Andalusia has acquired a total of eight hundred copies of the "Diary de un Poeta Reciencasado", curated by Pietro Tabernero and illustrated by James Perez-Enciso, to continue the diffusion of the figure and the work of one of Andalusian poets of highest value.
