Speak in the Sleep
Some of the kids talk at the night.
“I’m not referring to those who wake up very agitated to tell us about something that happened to them the previous day, but to those children who speak while remaining asleep.
The first time it happens, at the most gets a scare, but in reality it doesn’t really matter.
It’s a benign sleep-abultimization called somniloquy.
It manifests with a certain frequency, especially in children and adolescents.
Definitely, most of you have said that you had spoken in your sleep.
This phenomenon can occur very sporaradically (once in a lifetime, e.g. associated with an episode of high fever) or with greater regularity (every day, once a week, once a month ...), the day after the baby no longer remembers anything.
There isn’t a single reason to explain this phenomenon.
They’re sometimes episodes related to other sleep disturbances such as nightmares, nocturnal fears (feeing into bed), or somnambulism, but that doesn’t necessarily be the case.
As already mentioned above, it can be associated with very high fever stadistances, stressful situations or over-excitation.
In any case, it’s still a little-studied phenomenon.
Intensity with which it appears is also extremely variable.
It can happen that the person speaks with a very calm tone of voice, with loose words, almost in a whisper or the opposite happens (screams, cries, long speeches ...).
In cases in which the event is particularly intense, it would be appropriate to look at the child and possibly consult a specialist to check if there is any question that is generating an anomalous state of anxiety as an outburst to the situation.
We’ve said it’s a benign alteration so there isn’t any treatment unless it’s associated with other sleep disorders that can be treated (e.g., when paired with insomnia or stress).
What can be done, however, is to take into consideration a series of very simple preventive measures such as:
Try not excusing the child excessively before he goes to sleep.
Propose quiet games and avoid programs or TV series that arouse fear or suggest violence.
With very nervous children for whom it is strenuous to unlease, relaxation techniques can be used to help them acquire some inner calm.
This will allow your child to sleep more quietly.
Once so-sloquions appear, try to stay calm.
Remind that they are inoffentrivial to the child and that the next day he won’t remember anything about what happened.
Useful Advice
- Don't wake him.
It’s fustile because he’s unacware of what’s going on and you’d interrupt his sleep.
Although he's talking, he's sleeping, so he's resting.
Remind that if it seems very distressed and the episodes have a certain frequency it is better to consult a doctor or check where such a great anxiety in the child comes from.
If one understands what he says (which is not the most common situation), don’t fall into the blemishness of putting an interrogation on him to get information.
You’ll get nothing, because he’s asleep and therefore unconsciential to organize his thinking.
In addition, you’re able to hear what it says but don’t see what he’s dreaming about, so you have to pay a lot of attention to the interpretation of the information and the conclusions they might bring.
Don’t ever tell the child that you could ask him questions at night to know his secrets.
Not even as a jest of as a joke.
Such a thing provokes enormous anguish.
In short, don’t worry, it’s nothing serious, it’s an episode that only lasts a few minutes.
