Sarkozy Meets Fishermen in Astray
Before leaving for the U.S., Nicolas Sarkozy will make a stop in Brittany.
In front of the demonstration of the fishermen who brutally prevented access to several Breton and Norman ports on Monday, the President of the Republic has decided to go Tuesday morning to the port of Guilvinec, in the department of Finistère, to enchant its representatives.
The latter have vowed to welcome it "with a constructive spirit" and they hope that "it presents itself with concrete proposals".
A presidential visit that takes place just at the same time as the General Assembly of the "Crisis Committee", created by Breton fishermen in order to take measures to cope with the surge in the price of diesel, which has reached its record with an average of more than 1.14 euros per liter.
Elysée spokeswoman David Martinon said: “The president will discuss with them about possible solutions to help businesses re-rebuiltiably their competitiveness, and to secure the working conditions and pay of sailors.”
Fishermen await Nicolas Sarkozy at a standstill.
On Tuesday morning, they conducted "snail operations" in Morbihan and Calvados, causing almost 10 km of traffic jam.
Quiberon’s port has also been shredded, but connections to Belle-Ale have not been disrupted.
On the Quiberon peninsula a roadblock was carried out.
On Monday the sailors have already shown their determination burning, starting from the sunrise, tires and shovels in front of fuel depots at the ports of Brest and Lorient, while demonstrations took hold in Concarneau and Saint-Brieuc.
The movement has spread towards the ports of the Atlantic and English Channel coasts, such as Sables d'Olonne, in the Vendée, where the fishing boats have remained at the pier, or in Havre, where about twenty boats have prevented the movement of commercial ships.
La Turballe et del Croisic’s fishermen at the Loir-Atlantique interrupted in the evening the block of the well-known Refinery in Donges.
