Remnant - The Blood Is Safe Again
The Héma-Québec celebrated the ten-fifteenth anniversary of its inception yesterday, and its president, Dr. FrancineDécary, had every reason to rejoice, as the Heéma-Québec has become one of the safest and most effective blood suppliers in the world.
And above all, it regained the public’s trust, seriously shaken by the scandal of infected blood in the years between 1980 and 1990.
As we all know, National Blood Reserves Managed by the Canadian Redcruicrusady Transfusion Service were infected with acquired immunodeficiency (HIV) and Hepatitis-Kestil in the 1980s.
Faced with such a tragedy and population protests, the Government of Canada in February 1994 conducted a public investigation, headed by Judge Horace Krever, which aimed to shed light on the circumstances that led to such a tragedy and to issue recommendations to prevent the re-archasing re-armarry of such a disaster.
While Judge Krever was slow to draw up a report, some provinces set up a committee with the aim of devising a re-evaluating blood management system in Canada.
In Québec, the Gélineau mandate was carried out by the Gélineau Committee.
Following Gélineau et Krever reports in Canada two suppliers have been set up: Héma-Québec to supply Québec and Société Canadienne du sang (Canadian Blood Society) for all other provinces and territories.
In 1998, when Héma-Québec started its activities, just 38% of people said they still had confidence in the Donation System in Québec.
The situation was mortifying.
Thanks to the continuous efforts to improve the safety of blood withdrawals and on-the-ground research over the following years, we have regained the trust of the public", he explains before he proudly states that at the beginning of 2008, about 94% of Québec's population now had confidence in the Shema-Québec.
Dr Décary is nevertheless convinced that "confidence is something extremely fragile".
Since its in-arrivement on 28 September 1998 the Héma-Québec has applied the precautionary principle.
At the time, there were numerous cases in Europe of the human variant of mad cow, a new manifestation of Creutzfeldt-Jakob's clergy disease, he recounts who has been chaired since 1998.
“And we didn’t know at all whether that disease could be transmitted through infected blood.
It is therefore based on one of the main recommendations of the Krever and Gélinau ratios, according to which when one has doubts and a serious disease could be achieved, in this case Creutzfeldt-Jakob's disease that manifests itself with a degeneration of the brain that inevitably leads to dis-death, exclusionary measures can be taken.
In April 1999, the blood of those who had spent more than a month in the United Kingdom has been rejected since 1980.
Now, for a few years, it has been proven that without a doubt this disease is transmitted through infected blood.
"This episode has thus shown that it made sense to apply the precautionary precautionary principle."
A much more-efficulent technique for the identification of HIV appeared on the market in 1999: nucleic acid (TAN) tests that allow the presence of the virus to be recognized directly in the blood of donors and not that of antibodies against it, on which the tests used until then were based.
The TANs have made it possible to reduce the window period, i.e. from when the person has contracted the disease to that in which the first viruses can be detected in the blood thanks to our techniques, explains the specialist.
That period could be reduced to less than a week.
The risk of ignoring the presence of infected donors and therefore not being able to detect them has thus decreased significantly.
These improvements made it possible to considerably decrease the risk of getting AIDS with a blood transfusion.
Today, one out of 12 million people run the risk of receiving infected blood from the HIV virus.
“In other words, this means you’re going to need to distribute 12 million blood samples before someone becomes infected with HIV.
Since Héma-Québec is distributing between 400,000 and 500,000 blood samples every year, it will be more than 24 years before a case of HIV transmitted with a transsubfusion occurs.
"I believe that today we can feel comfortable receiving or prescribing a blood transliption to recover health, says the head of the management of the Shema-Québec.
"Our challenge for the next decade is to maintain the safety of our products.
We never know what pathogen will emerge in the system.
Not more than a year ago the Chikungunya virus appeared on Réunion Island which, like the West Nile virus, is transmitted in blood by an insect.
You have to be constantly vigilant", continues Dr. Décary before pointing out that, in addition to having safe resources, the Héma-Québec must also provide enough.
“Descreases increase as the population ages.
Being the most aggressive cancer treatments, patients often need an intake of red blood cells or platelets.
Eagleging the population also has an impact on the number of donors which on average make up only 3% of Québec’s inhabitants acceptable as donors.”
However, all healthy people who are over the age-of-the-year-old who meet the eligibility criteria can donate their blood every 56 days, i.e. six times a year.
On average, 14% of people who offer themselves as donors aren’t accepted because they are suffering from AIDS or Malaria, e.g., and if they’ve traveled to a country where it’s widespread, they’ll be turned down for a period of one year.
“Throximately with this 3% we are able to maintain an inventory capable of responding to the needs of hospitals.
Catch cases are very uncomplicated.
We need to re-renovate the members of this group of donors as they get older and can’t make donations anymore.
“Now it’s difficult to convince people to donate blood, but above all to restrain them and incise them to become regular donors.
“Fourth day life is so hecquited that it’s hardly an hour to donate blood,” Francine Décary desolately says.
With an annual budget of $297 million, Héma-Québec not only distributes blood samples, but also human tissues, of which bone transplants, olcular-gloomy heart valves and skin that is used almost exclusively for severe burns.
These streaks are analysed, transformed, and frozen before being deposited at the Héma-Québec, which manages the only public umbilical cot blood bank in Québec.
In the umbilical chorus, which was once considered bio-medical waste, absuffered blood rich in stem cells that can be used to cure serious diseases, such as leukemia.
“A few weeks ago, a few weeks ago, the inclusion in the bank of our thousand-thousor sample of cord blood and we plan to withdraw more than a thousand every year.
We should begin to propose them for transplants at the beginning of next year", says Dr. Décary, who describes this practice of bottling cord blood as very popular.
"Many mothers are disappointed not to be able to have access", as the Héma-Québec does not dispose of the necessary means to propose this complex and expensive program in all hospitals in Québec.
