"Expanding the boundaries of our last great northern frontier": this is the long-term ambition of Jean Charest, presented yesterday in Levis, by means of instruments with a strong visual impact, on the occasion of the General Council of the Quebec Liberal Party.
Behind him, during his speech, the projection of a sort of "Model to Images".
Out of three maxi-cest screens, they merged the spectacular negatives of hydro-teatro dams, mineral crops, images of Quebec seen with "Google Maps", maps, etc.
Great means as in the middle of the election campaign.
During the press conference, the prime minister refused to exclude the start of the autumn elections, even if he pointed out that he was very busy.
"Northern Plan" is the fourth point of a "project" that the prime minister says has for Quebec, that of a "new economic space", which understands a first agreement with France on labor, a second agreement between Canada and Europe and other agreements with Ontario.
Approximately referred to in the Prime Minister's most recent speeches, the opening of the North to the 49th parallel - had never been the subject of a presentation.
The Prime Minister, however, confined himself to explaining why the North would be "a jewel with an immense potential", but did not pronountil in terms of timing or investment shares, just as he did not explain the precise priorities in the implementation of future projects.
At the end of course he limited himself to pointing out what in his opinion deserves a priority: hydro-electricity, forests, but above all the mining sector, which following the growth of emerging countries and the reconstruction of public infrastructure has experimented with a number of improvements.
According to Charest's list, the North contains "nickel, zinc, copper, platinum, gold, silver, iron, titan, and even diamonds".
That "big-greater territory than France", it's here, it's Quebec.
It’s not only here, but it’s inherent in us,” he insisted, like wanting to solicit a national founding myth.
Que Quebec's foundations are rooted in its relationship with the North, he pointed out: gold fever in Abitibi, National Engineering, James Bay.
"It's up to us, it's our future", he added, after a theatrical pause to contemplate the immense map behind him.
“The North, 70 per cent of Quebec’s territory, includes a part of Labrador that Quebec has always claimed, the prime minister said at a press conference.
“It’s a stance traditionally maintained by all governments.
"There is a border line on which there has been no agreement for a long time and, when the opportunity presents itself, I will reiterate that position in the name of the government," he indicated.
“Concerning climate change, we must re-evaluate the North,” he said, speaking of the north-west passage, which will be open throughout the year due to the melting of the ice.
"In this new geopolitical context, the Prime Minister estimates that occupying the territory is a duty."
What about the locals, the First Nations and the Inuit ?
“The Prime Minister has said that the government of Quebec would be very careful in this regard and that the project would meet their interests, especially those of young people.
Two ex-ministers coming from different parties, Pierre Corbeil and Michel Létourneau (ex-committee of FP d'Ungava), have already kick-started the consultation of about thirty communities.
An inter-ministerial committee chaired by Benoit Pelletier and in which Julie Boulet (Transport and Natural Resources) occupies an important position, has started work.
“The prime minister doesn’t rule out a big meeting, “not necessarily a summit”, but a time when “common visions will be provided.”
The development of the North would subsequently stick to the principles of sustainable turmoil.
“The North Plan will be “compared, orderly and will respect the environment,” he vowed.
Jean-charest described this "project" as a continuum with that of the liberal prime ministers who preceded it.
“Like the pre-previous liberals, we know how to stand out to transform challenges into new opportunities for growth and development.”
This was also one of the recurrential topics of the weekend, which began on Friday with a great cocktail in honor of the 50th anniversary of Jean Lesage’s arrival as head of the party.
One of the adopted resolutions proposes rebaptizing Central Beauharnois in Digial Adélard-Godbout.
“From Godbout, the creator of Hydro-Québec to Lesage nationalized hydroelectricity in Bourassa, the creator of James Bay, our party has always been able to forge the future,” said Jean Charest.
During the press conference, he resorted to another, Conservative reference this time, John Diefenbaker's "Strengthening Roads to the North" program of John Diefenbaker, Prime Minister from 1957 to 1963.
Facilitating the project related to the hydroelectric dam of Eastmain1/a-Ruper, Charest has clarified that its putting into operation would be previewed for 2012.
"All this remains among us, but I feel that we will succeed," he confessed to his militants, arousing applause.
In fact, the Prime Minister, who said there would have been no elections in 2008 and probably not even in 2009, did not dismantle the possibility of starting an ballot in the autumn, which could happen in accordance with the law.
Emphasizes that he is certainly very busy, particularly on a federal council mission in China and the Quebec-New York summit.
The last weekend's General Council provided the liberal leader with a platform for nationalist resolutions, in particular "constitute aspirations" that have passed smoothly like oil: PLQ mainly calls for the recognition of "Quebec-specificity" in the Constitution.
Pursuant Quebec to appoint members of the Canadian Broadcasting and Telecommunications Council members, and the latter have a veto of decisions made in Quebec.
As far as the opposition is concerned, the sketch of the North Plan has been skeptically welcomed.
PQ leader Pauline Marois dusted off the Northern Quebec Development Policy launched by the Landry government in 2001.
According to her the liberal government, which came to power in 2003, did not respect its application.
"We would have progressed today", Marois commented alongside the Jean-Talon (scrutinium that took place today).
The ADAQ has said it supports the adoption of the North Plan by the government.
"What we hope is that all this will not simply be perceived as a show", commented leader Sébastien Proulx, Mario Dumont's replacement, on his return today from the holidays.
Lectures - A disgraced country
It 'was Vic Toews, ex-Minister of "Jourtish" (Ironically of Looting) of the Harper cabinet, who allowed himself to declare last summer that Louise Arbor was a "shame" for Canada.
Dishonor in this way a woman who has incessantly fought for justice and who deserves all our admiration says a lot about both the Prime Minister's entourage and Stephen Harper himself.
Here we are in the face of a government that is the only one, among all the Western countries involved, who did not repatriate one of its imprisoned citizens in the Guantanamo concentration camp.
A government that doesn’t take into account the fact that Omar Khadr was a simple teen at the time the imputing facts happened.
The only government that, despite having signed the Geneva Convention, renegees its own word and lets a child-soldier rot in inhumane conditions.
The only one who disfies in a parody of justice in order to establish the culpability.
We would like to point out that  UNICEF, Amnesty International and the Order of Canadian Lawyers, in particular, call for the rehearsal of Omar Khadra in order to be subjected to a regular trial in Canada.
It is just that same government that closes its eyes in front of torture: it cares very little that a young adult is now psychologically devastated, perhaps forever, after so many years of abuse.
She doesn’t realize it yet, but we’re seeing an insidious erosion of our rights—de-mocracy—a Speyer that Harper doesn’t ceasing to flaunt on her lips.
Here we are at the moment when there is no longer any morality: one can mistreat, torture, arbitrarily imprison a Canadian citizen according to the discretion of a head-of-govern until now a juridence (to see what will become after October 14...).
Numerous electors believe that a party that preaches Virtuous, Responsions of the Law and the Order represents salvation: they don’t see that this government has renounced what constituted the reputation and dignity of our country.
It's right here the disgrace, Mr. Toews in renouncing all this, and nowhere else.
Unletter: The Orwellian Speech of Barrick Gold
Le Devoir published a letter by M. Patrick J. Garver, vice-president director of the Canadian mining company Barrick Gold.
Pursuing an Orwellian logic, the latter states that the largest ariferous company in the world has filed a defamation lawsuit of six million against three perpetrators and a pengrin small publishing house with the intention of initiating a "transparent public debatation".
“Whether it’s true that the judges aren’t independent and unbiased, it’s not the truth they’re judging, especially in the case of defamination lawsuit, but the law.
Unsurprinment, this substantial difference is completely concealed by Garver.
Since for Barrick Gold, the best way to conduct a public debate would be to ruin anyone who dares to support a stance contrary to her.
Nam no matter the outcome of the trial, the writers and the editor of the Nero Canada book will have to spend a substantial amount of money in order to organize their own defense and fight, decisively unherited weapons, against an army of lawyers financed by the practically bottomless pockets of the mining company.
After all, in these relentless gunfights, the tactic is often to ruin the defendant in order to impose a consensual regulation that muzzles the opposition in addition to frightening anyone or pronouncing themselves again on the issue.
“Whether Barrick Gold really wanted a public debate, it’s in front of the public that she would have to present her vision of the facts.
Contrary to what happens in the many countries where the Barrick Gold works, in Canada the debates normally take place with the exchange of opinions and not with truncheons.
It would have therefore been enough for the company to open its books, its documents, and establish a real debate, through newspapers, media, publications and the funding of teams of independent researchers who could shed light on the issue.
But Barrick Gold doesn’t seem to have as much confidence in public opinion and misrepely rejects the petitions and letters in support of the perpetrators of Black Canada.
Perhaps Garver could explain how he can ask for “public” debate when his disdain for that same audience is evident?
In vice-president's world, it seems that this word refers exclusively to lawyers handsomely handsomely paid and not the whole of the Canadians; here's a rather narrow view of the concept.
In a democratic system, non-denigrators have the right to speak.
“What Barrack Gold doesn’t understand—woman—would not understand—entres are that the audience that upholds the authors and publishers of Black’s Canada does primarily because it rejects the company’s fifties.
This audience is ready to listen to the Barrick and to judicate, but on the basis of civilian exchanges of opinion, and not in the perspective of what many perceive as a sort of gagging (regarding the "taplied debatation", the Barrick Gold has just given the authors of Canada Nero an ultimatum by ordering them to suspend the utilisation of that word...).
Beyond Garver’s unreligation discourse, the lawsuit filed by Barrack Gold constitutes a direct attack on the freedom of university research and the pursuit of the truth, indispensable in every democratic society.
It denies en shrewd the right to citation of authentic sources and the debate on facts and shroutures, which form the very basis of intellectual work.
“Whether it’s impossible in a country like Canada to study and discuss uncomfortable topics for the world’s wealthiest businesses, pity multiple lawsuits, who can do that?
“Whether Barrick Gold really wanted a public and transparent debate, they could do it according to scientific norms as the perpetrators of Black Canada do.
He's got a rehearsal.
For a very long time, the university environment, of which we are a part, is able to manage debates and disagreements and resolve conflicts.
Barrick Gold’s excessive measures clearly show that, contrary to its claims, it does not seek a transparent debate at all.
This comes at the expense of academia, just as the public debate in Canada is affected, and it’s time for governments to take measures that can limit unfair lawsuits.
