The judgment of Google is therefore rather contradictory: those who are afraid to provide their personal data hurrize Google because they believe that in this way their intimate sphere and private life is affected by starting to use its services and are lifted with the rebellion due to the multinationals that stand out for the conservative image.
In the meantime the geek American culture and the proliferation of enthusiasts, activists and computer users appeared in numerous countries see in them the next generation of large companies, the "cool corporation", as opposed to their rival, Microsoft liquidated as conquerors of the world due to Windows, although they both fight for the same goal.
This can happen because Google, unlike Microsoft, does not ask its users for an identification code, does not ask for money for their services, the source code always kept up-to-date and the package of programs is much more accessible, youthful and cool than the official practice of the software giant.
But it can also happen also because geeks see exactly the problematic relationship between personal rights and the internet, which implies precisely one of the key words repeated most often in recent years, web 2.0.
The web 2.0 speaks of nothing but of the collaboration, to intentionally leave traces behind us and take advantage of the communicative channels at our disposal and while the size of these changes for each individual, the substance is the same: to show ourselves.
An average young person can create profiles in multiple community pages, from Facebook to Xanga, he can connect them with other services such as Twitter or last.fm that lists his musical tastes, all of these can insert them into systems such as FriendFeed or Blip.fm, he can also write blogs on which his most recent photo galleries automatically appear on Flickr or Picasa.
In the meantime we read of numerous exemplary cases from which it is clear that the girl, seeing the series of photos taken in the morning by the common tour of friends, comes to know easily with those who have spent at the night her perfidious boy or a company can fire an employee because she has written a sincere opinion on the top of the company: one thing is sure, the concept of private life has now completely reconsidered.
Those who live an on-the-line life make sure to show themselves and in this way we can formulate the fundamental the thesis that hides behind the behavior of young people.
Those who want to hide something or only want to avoid the monopoly of Google can (starting for example with Scroogle using the search system of Google but that at the same time does not voluntarily leave traces).
There aren’t a lot of people who don’t account purposely on community pages so they won’t be found, on-line Google’s applications, from word processing to image management and newsgaze, they can be substituted with alternative versions not related to huge server parks and that don’t compose our best on-existing profile so far.
Public opinion awaits even so much in secret at the moment when Google pulls its claws out.
