The Warsaw Stock Exchange has made an offer to purchase the Prague Stock Exchange
The Warsaw Stock Exchange has offered 200 million euros, about 5 billion Czech kroner, for the purchase of 100% of the share price of the Stock Exchange in Prague.
Ludwik Sobolewski, president of the Warsaw Stock Exchange, has confirmed the offer made by the Poles although the Czechs had previously ruled out the Warsaw Stock Exchange as a possible buyer of the Prague Stock Exchange.
The reason for the exclusion is the fact that the Warsaw Stock Exchange is state-owned.
The 99% of the shares are in the hands of the Polish state that it intends to sell within a few months more than 60% of its securities.
Czech financial newspaper E15 states that the offer of Poles represents the highest figure so far advanced for the Prague Stock Exchange.
According to the Czech press so far the Vienna Stock Exchange, the Nasdaq Transatlantic Stock Exchange, the New York Stock Exchange, the London Stock Exchange and the German Stock Exchange.
Petr Koblic, Director of the Stock Exchange in Prague, said last week that among all those interested the Warsaw Stock Exchange would be the Wrist Owner and didn’t rule out that this could lead to the closure of the Prague Stock Exchange in the future.
Market experts estimate the value of the Prague Stock Exchange in 5-10 billion kroner.
According to the shareholder agreement, almost 90% of the shares would be sold.
The largest owner of the Prague Stock Exchange is Patria-Comrade with a share of almost 25%, followed by ?esko Spo?itelna with 14.7% and the Tiger Holding Four S. a.r.l. with 13.5%
