Enthus imitation hornet's flight
What could be the best method to develop anti-collision technologies other than mimimicking hurdles of obstacles in nature?
For example those of bees.
In the start-breaking project presented at CEATEC in Japan hornets gave the initial strategic cue to Nissan’s engineers on how to build the next-generation anti-collision systems.
Combined with the University of Tokio, Nissan built the biomimetic robot BR23C, a robotic mini-car equipped with bee-shared properties, with the ultimate aim of creating a system capable of shuffling all collisions.
“Flying the bees form its own oval personal space that really resembles our protective shield,” Kazuhiro Doi, director of PR and R&D technologies, told the exhibition.
Very important is the role of bee-sight, capable of a 300-degree view in every direction.
The engineers drew on the telemetric laser idea to copy the bee vision function.
The BR23C detects hurdles up to two yards within a radius of 180 degrees, calculates its distance and sends a signal to the on-board microprocessor that immediately transforms it into a command to avoid a collision.
In a fraction of a second the robot perceives the hurdle and then, imitating the movements of the bee, immediately changes direction by turning the repellers of the measure necessary to avoid the accident.
This avoidance manoeuvring, in a different way from the current system, takes place in a completely instinctive way.
“Whether it wasn’t the robot car he wouldn’t be able to react quickly in order to avoid collisions,” explains Toshiyuki Andoh, Director of the Motiability Laboratory and the Auto Robot Project.
It must react instinctively and immediately because this technology corresponds to the damageable and innermost layer of the protective shield, within which the collision is currently considered unavoidable.
The whole process has to mirror what a bee does to avoid the other bees.
This process must take place in the blink of an eye.
But unlike bees the deflection can’t go up or down or diagonal, it can only be two-square and in the direction of sterzability of the wheels.
In this way instead of the infinite possibilities available for bees to shun the other bees, the robot, as a possibility available, can rotate on itself, accelerate and slow down.
The BR23C is powered by lithium-milimer batteries, lighter, with high-density energy density, which means their lifespan is greater than lithium-ion batteries.
According to Andoh, the iststantiality of the intelligent reaction is important, not the processing of the data or even less the ability to store it.
This robot has to process a few inputs in a matter of seconds and react.
You don’t have to memorize them and react with intuitive logic based on the previous maneuvering data, so it only uses a memory of a few tens of Kb.
You don’t need a gigabyte/megabyte or mega-to-memory.
This is the first step towards the development of a technology for the immediate avoidance of the collisions that, they predict at Nissan, within 10 years it will be able to be installed on production vehicles.
An additional possibility of application can be unification with the GPS satellite navigation system which, avoiding the obstacles on the road leads to its destination.
They have also found the name to the very expensive child.
