Industry prices continue to rise
The prices of domestic industrial sales in August 2008 increased by 0.1% compared to the previous month, by 12.9% compared to August 2007 - informs the Central Institute of Hungarian Statistics.
The export sales prices of the industry compared to July 2008 were 1.1% higher, compared to the previous year they fell by 3.9%
As a result of the change in domestic and export price, industrial production costs were 0. 7% higher than in the previous month and 3.2% compared to a year ago.
The domestic industrial sales prices for the previous month increased by only 0.1%.
The domestic sales prices of the processing sector also rose to a minimum of 0.1%, compared to July.
The largest increase in prices in August 2008 occurred in the field of leather and shoe processing (2.8%), especially handbags with 4. , 6.6 percent and shoe production with 2.5% monthly increase.
The high price level of plastic and chemical raw materials caused a significant monthly increase (1.6%) in products and chemicals.
A decrease in prices took place in the field of oil processing (1.4%), in the production of electrical instruments and machinery (0.9%) and to a minimal extent in wood processing (0.1%).
The prices of paper production, editorial and typographical activities have not changed.
There has been an increase in prices between 0.2 and 0. 9% in the other sectors of the working-class industry.
In the field of the supply of electricity, gas, vapour and water, prices increased in August by 0.3% in national industrial sales, primarily as a result of the prevailing effect of the increase in the gas price carried out by the authorities from 1 July.
Compared over 12 months, taking into account the domestic sales of the processing sectors of the working industry, the largest increases took place in the field of oil processing (32.4%).
In the field of the manufacture of the products deriving from the processing of metals, the increase in prices was 13.2%, 11% as part of the production of food, beverages and tobacco representing a decisive weight.
A decrease in prices took place in the textile sector (2.5%) and in the production of electric tools and machinery (0.3%).
In the other sectors of the machining industry the pace of the price increase varied from 0.6% to 9.1%.
In total in the processing industry in the past year, prices rose by 10.2%.
Prices of the electricity, gas, steam and water supply sectors exceeded last year by 18.7%.
In domestic sales among target groups in sectors, the largest increase in monthly prices took place in August in sectors producing investment goods (0.7%).
In sectors that produce consumer goods, prices have risen by 0.3%, prices have not changed compared to those of the previous month in the energy and subsequent utilisation sectors.
In August 2008, compared to the same month of last year, the largest increase in prices was observed in the energy and subsequent utilisation sectors (16.8%).
A decrease of 1.4% was in the sectors producing consumer articles the increase was 6. . . . . . .
The prices of industrial export sales calculated in guilders grew by 1.1% in August compared to the previous month, compared to 12 months they decreased by 3.9%.
The Hungarian lendor in a month weakened against the euro by 1.7% against the dollar of 7.1%, compared to August 2007, compared with August 2007 it strengthened by 7.5% against the euro and by 15.9% against the dollar.
