Juraka: The vicious circle of poverty and segregation must be broken
The European Equal Opportunities Summit has begun today in Paris, organized this year by France with the rotating Presidency of the European Union.
Fidesz deputy of the European People's Party in the message addressed to the plenum as the main speaker of the congress has drawn attention to school segregation because it is the most serious legal violation that affects Roma children and moreover the most serious obstacle for their affirmation.
Mr Xavier Bertrand French Minister for Employment and Social Affairs, on behalf of the French Presidency, and the European Commissioner Vladim ?pidla responsible for Employment, Social Affairs and Equal Opportunities opened the conference at the highest level.
One of those invited to the conference were governments, European social partners and representatives of the major European civil organisations and the defence of equal-equal opportunity rights.
According to the Press Office of the Fidesz PE Parliamentary Group (Jaraka), he recalled, in addition to discrimination in recruitment, that Roma are ousted from the labor market in the first place because of their low schooling.
"The majority of young Roma do not have an average-of-the-speaker degree and as a result of poor-quality elementary teaching only a negligible number of them can reach a higher degree."
He also emphasised the key role of education as a fundamental human right in enforcing human rights.
“Endangering in the education of Roma children can mean the strengthening of the economy of EU member states.
According to the congresswowowowowowowowowoman, it is necessary to make these links in the wider area between the Roma and non-Roma.
In fact, the sooner a Roma a school begins, the more its chances of becoming a productive member of the labor market grow; through taxes paid for income and consumption, they contribute to the budget of the nation.
"The right of European citizenship of the Roma must guarantee their right to quality education and the elimination of low-level and segregated schools," he said.
According to the congresswowowowowowowoman is necessary the unity of purpose and action of the European Union in order to speed up the desecrimination processes of the schools and for this it is of paramount importance the right compliance with existing and planned anti-discrimination directives.
"The vicious circle of poverty, segregation and unemployment must be interrupted, it is necessary to give Roma children the opportunities afforded by the democratic space so that they can contribute as full-fledged citizens of the future of Europe."
In the sign of the "European Year of Equal Opportunities" to be guaranteed for all, the aim of the European Equal Opportunities Summit organized every year since the Berlin meeting last year is to ensure a unique forum to promote the anti-Discrimination and Equal Opportunities objectives set at State and European level.
