As opposed to an opposition politician, Russian observers judged elections democratic.
Sergei Lebedev, president of the Commonwealth of Independent States, told RIA Novosti: "the men believe not in words but in deeds", so the lack of opposition is none other than proof of maturity of the Belarusian voters.
Brunetta: "From now on breakfast before the work"
It has never happened in the history of Palazzo Chigi that there were revolving doors for the entry and exit of employees.
But from today starts this new measure against the cunning, against those who time and then go for a coffee at the bar during working hours.
The anti-infnulloni innovation was presented today by the Minister of the Public Administration, Renato Brunetta, and the Secretary-General of the Presidency of the Council, Mauro Masi.
Revolving doors - says Brunetta - are a tool to work better, with more productivity, more presences and more services", specifies the minister.
“Now a new season of accountasurability and transparency is opening.
“That’s reformism,” Brunetta points out.
With Palazzo Chigi the installation is completed in all the buildings of the Presidency of the Council.
For Masi "the choice of swivel doors is also important from a symbolic point of view".
“There has been no resistance on the part of the employees, the secretary-general assures: “It has been a process shared by the workers and there has been everyone’s cooperation”.
In short, no more breakfast in working hours; no more coffee and croissant.
The words of Brunetta, who suggests, smiling, "a nice cappuccino at 7 in the morning is a holy hand".
In the course of a conference at the Higher School of the Public Administration
of Rome, the minister then announces that "in three months the absences due to illness of civil servants have been reduced by 50%, i.e. 60,000 more people at work in a year".
And he adds that it is necessary to make the Public Administration more efficient
Italian, "whose wage dynamics - explains the minister - in the last 8-10 years have been double compared to the private sector and well above inflation rates".
In the meantime, thousands of civil servants have come together in order to contest the norms of the Brunetta decree adjoining the recurrence supported by Codacons.
"While the Minister of the Public Function today flaunters numbers about the least absenteeism at work due to causes of illness - writes the consumer association - the people of 'inperculous scuffles' are constantly growing.
In fact, there are a number of correct and productive employees of the public administration in Italy who have joined together to declare war on the illegitimate norms contained in the decree".
Particular in particular, the TAR will be contested the exclusion of additional economic treatments for infirmity depending on cause of service; the extension of the time slots of availability of the sick worker, within which the check-ups are carried out, and other aspects contained in the new provisions desired by the minister.
Work accidents, Napolitano: "Cromindial prevention"
Every day 2,500 accidents occur at work in Italy, 3 people die and 27 remain permanently disabled.
For 2007, the Inail data confirm that there were about 1,200 white deaths.
Figures that testify to the seriousness of a phenomenon that remains one of the main causes of death and causes almost twice the number of fatalities compared to homicides.
Removing the attention of institutions, social forces and the media on this dramatic issue, today Anmil celebrates the 58th National Day for the Victims Victims of Accidents at Work in all the provinces of Italy.
"The troubling data disseminated by Anmil and the same tragic chronicles of these days confirm how crucial the issue of prevention at work is", says the President of the Republic Giorgio Napolitano, in the message sent to the President of Anmil, Mutilated National Association and invalid of work, Pietro Mercandelli.
The head of state expresses his "living appreciation for the constant associative commitment in favor of prevention in the workplace, the protection of injured workers, the assistance of the families of the victims and the awareness-raising of public opinion".
"There has risen, of course, an indignant "enough", sincerely shared, in the face of tragedies that, because of their size, arouse the clamor of the media and the involvement of public opinion but the daily reality re-proposes dramatic cases, even repetitive in their dynamics".
Napolitano doesn't hide that "there is undoubtedly also a problem of resources: it is crucial to invest in training and information.
Particular significance takes on the numerous initiatives promoted in the educational field for a more direct awareness on the part of young people entering the world of work.
It is necessary to keep alive the attention to the phenomenon, do not wail in the alarm about its social seriousness, to apply and to improve legislative rules.
For its part, the President of the Chamber Gianfranco Fini underlined "how the phenomenon of white deaths constitutes an absolute social emergency, which offends the conscience of each of us and imposes on all the institutions a decisive commitment aimed at urgently reaching an end".
"The data on accidents at work seem to signal a slight positive trend, but this does not mean that the number of white deaths is still impressive", said the president of Anmil Pietro Mercandelli.
"We do not wish, in fact, that this "downward trend" is translated into less attention, because it is precisely when the attention loosens that the tragedy explodes.
"We believe that on a general level too little is still done to counter the phenomenon of accidents and the contrast can be exerted through manifold actions.
In our opinion, greater checks and inspections are necessary, but also a stronger intervention on the training of the workers, and this starting from the world of the school".
"We need a further effort - he has added - with regard to the communication and awareness of public opinion.
“But above all we need a greater sense of responsibilisity on the part of employers towards a phenomenon that represents a social emergency”.
Mercandelli then denounced "the progressive deterioration in levels of indemnified protection provided for the victims of work" and the "caspray assistance to which the system is pushing in recent years".
"The objective is therefore to return to the worker the insurance guarantee - he added - because the fate of those who get injured should not depend on spontaneisms or donation from time to time disposed from the legislature".
Mp3 readers under accusation, put hearing at risk
Hearing high-volume music for an extended period of time using portable music devices such as MP3 players can cause permanent hearing injuries.
This is reported by the European Commission, which in a communiqué released in Brussels cites the opinion of the Scientific Committee on Emerging and Recent-Recognized Health Risks (Csrseri) of the EU, published today.
"From this scientific opinion it emerges that 5-10% of those who, for a period of at least 5 years, listen to music with portable musical devices weekly for more than an hour a day keeping the device at a high volume risk a permanent loss of hearing capacity".
“I’m worrying the fact that such a large number of juveniles who frequently use hand-to-capa portable music devices and mobile phones at high audible audible levels can subconsciously damage their hearing,” says Meglena Kuneva.
"From the scientific finds - continues the EU's head - a clear risk emerges and we need to react quickly.
Most importantly, we need to sensitize consumers and make this information in the public domain.
“We also need to re-examine the existing control systems, in the light of this scientific opinion, in order for them to be fully effective and keep up with new technology.”
Brussels had asked the Independent Scientific Committee to look at the problem, in view of the great use of portable music devices and the increase in the number of young people exposed to noise.
"Scientists confirm that there is a cause for concern and the European Commission will now examine together with Member States and interested parties what measures could possibly be taken to better protect children and adolescents from exposure to noise from portable music devices and similar equipment".
At the moment, there is already a European safety standard that limits the noise level of portable musical equipment to 100 dB, but there is growing concerns about hearing damage that may result from overexposure to these sources of noise.
It is possible to significantly limit this damage by resorting to solutions such as the reduction of levels and the duration of exposure to noise.
In its opinion, the EU Scientific Committee points out that users of portable music equipment if they listen at a high volume (more than 89 decibels) music for only 5 hours per week exceeds the limits currently in force related to the maximum noise allowed in the workplace.
Those who listen to music in such conditions for more prolonged periods face a permanent loss of auditory capacity after a 5-weeen period.
This would affect 5-10% of the users of these devices and their number can be quantified between 2.5 and 10 million people in the EU.
Rome, Alemanno starts the Commission for the future of the capital
Today it begins, in March it closes, in June the sums will be drawn.
This is the calendar set by the Campidoglio for the works of the "Committee for the future of Roma Capitale", which took office this morning in its new headquarters behind the Baths of Caracalla.
In six months, President Antonio Marzano and the other 49 members of the Commission will fine-tune the guidelines for Rome.
Once its doors are closed in March, the Junta and the City Council will evaluate analyses and projects, in order to be able to present the Strategic Plan for Rome with the "Official stamp" of the Campidoglio and its mayor Gianni Alemanno.
"The Commission is absolutely independent and all the political, social and cultural ideas are represented - Alemanno underlines - Alemanno - It will have to provide proposals for an idea of development in Rome of which the political decision-makers, who remain the Junta and the City Council, can then avail.
“There’s no superimposition of roles but only the will to have a place to develop a great project for Rome.
In March he will finish his work and then it will be dismantled, because this experience must have a precise temporal term.
In juridm - the mayor announce the mayor - we will present the strategic plan using the work done by the Commission for the future of Roma Capitale".
To those accusing the Commission of fut-to-fessary, since its opinions will not be binding, Alemanno replies: "It all depends on the work that the Commission will be able to do and on the quality of its proposals.
“Whether quality is there, as well as in France with the Aglylian Commission and as I’m also sure in Rome, its contribution will be crucial.
Too often the policy is crushed on the management of the newspaper and on the emergencies that from time to of time occur.
“We, on the contrary, want to look longer and farther, in a more profound perspective, working earnibly on the development of Rome and its future.”
In addition, the mayor of Rome wants to point out that "this Commission is neither a fuss nor an idle body: it is absolutely gratuitous, it will not cost the Roman citizens a euro and it will be used to reason and to involve the best intelligences expressed by the capital".
For Alemanno, in fact, "it is necessary to have a design of strategic reference, not closed or immovable, to allow our city to make an authentic leap in level, which it absolutely needs, thinking with a ununiting spirit of its future".
The mayor of the mayor of Rome "would not be confined only to the current municipal borders but will look far beyond" not only in the perspective of the future metropolitan city and its legal status as capital but "also in consideration of the role that Rome can and must play as the Capital of Europe and the Mediterranean".
