Elites in Danger
The husk had already manifested itself a few days ago with the House of Religation vote against the $700 billion bailout launched by the White House: a significant part of Americans was, and is, more interested in punishing Wall Street than in saving the economy.
The New York Times yesterday assures us that the current crisis is splitting the Republican Party, increasingly divided between its old East Coast soul, moderate, in favor of the federal institutions and the business community, and its new soul, instead conservative, hostile to "Waden of Washington "and the world of finance", strong especially in the States of the Central and the South.
The soul, in fact, who became overbearably alive with the vote of which I said at the beginning.
This just mentioned, however, is only an example of the changes, of the great changes, that the economic earthquake in progress perhaps heralds or already allows us to see: not only in the United States but throughout the West and perhaps not even here alone.
Both in the United States and in Europe the crisis seems to act as an accelerator of in incubation phenomena for some time that in the new climate are solidifying and finally coming to light.
The first of these phenomena is re-annoctualization, the extraordinary revitalization, of the two-fold state-sovereignty category in relation to a sort of renationalization of the economy.
The crisis, in fact, is the crisis of banking-to-financial institutions which have, yes, dense links with foreign countries, but which first of all involve the budgets of people and families who live in a single country, in a single state.
This immediately creates a political problem for those who govern it: namely how to respond to the difficulties and protests of those people and those families who, among other things, are also an electorate.
In short, the crisis appears economically viable but politically it is almost exclusively national.
Politiological internationalism seems essentially out of the game or does not have a lot to say: the clear proof is given by the European Union which divided as usual between the different interests and between the different state strategies fails to decide on any common political line.
And so it is from the government of every single State that everyone expects interventions, bailout and revital plans, new rules, and above all disbursement of funds: from the fact that when it comes to the brisk are only the states that possess the economic resources, the mass of financial resources capable of trying to put things back on track.
And they also have the necessary means and the legitimization to use them: two resources of immeasurable value, under certain circumstances, of which probably no market and no international organization can possibly dispose to a similar extent.
This new Emphantism that the crisis places on the nation-maceration is in perfect harmony with the ever-increasing importance that the most recent developments in the economy tend to attribute to a factor very closely connected to that element: territoriality.
Paradodoxically, in fact, while we were convinced that we have now entered the realm of the network, the increasingly sophisticated technology, of the immaterial, while we were convinced that globalized finance was now destined to dominate the world, we suddenly realized that our future will instead have to reckon to an increasing extent with those very unintangible things that are water, crops, oil.
All things that can certainly be transported from one place to the other of the earth but are still absolutely linked to a circumscribed space, to a territory.
So that those who find themselves sovereign, certainly have several more cards than those who are not, to those who have the misfortune to live in a place without crops, without oil and without water.
I’ll come to the second novelty that portends a rift.
Those who say Territorial, State, Government, inevitably say Politics, and therefore Leadership.
In this regard the economic crisis seems to produce two converging phenomena.
On the one hand the awareness of the objective need for authoritative leadership, the demand of someone who knows how to take matters into his own hands.
On the other hand a wave of disrelaxation for the ultensities of existing, economic species, proved so inadequate and full of shadow areas.
And at the same time something even more profound and in a certain disturbing sense: a disrectance, an impatience, an image of inadequacy, a sense of remoteness, which tends to involve the entire ruling class in an increasing number of countries in the West.
In other words it seems to make more and more way, in the vast sectors of the population, the conviction that before their actions are the very ideas of social elites so far in vogue, their way of feeling and being, their culture in the overall sense of the term, to have made their own time and to be increasingly extraneous to the opinions of majorities.
The demand for leadership, in short, slavishly elablisses a clarified but mighty delegitimization movement of the ruling classes and the traditional political structures, which manifests itself in the abducted "arrivolts "electors" or in the sudden turns of the collectivist moors of these last and very recent times, from the conservative rise in Austria-Baviera to the collapse of the Labour consensitizations in Great Britain.
One has the impression that the traditional elites, their parties, their programs, but their rits, their newspapers, their accredited intellectuals, their formats would say someone, are increasingly struggling to fully understand, and therefore to represent, what is not from today taking shape in the deep strata of Western societies and that the economic crisis impairs, increases, shakes potentially excessively.
In the face of all this talking about a "rebellion of the masses" on the agenda would frankly exaggerate.
But keeping your eyes wide open certainly isn’t at all.
Superglue to walk on the walls
Depreciate the laws of gravity thanks to a super-fennel and in the future walk on walls like Spider-Man.
It’s the goal of several American research centers and now a team of four institutes has been very close.
With an adhesive of only 2.5 square centimeters, the researchers managed to keep a 100-kilogram-kilogram-bodied body glued on a vertical wall.
THE STUDY - The American journal Science has published the findings of researchers from the University of Dayton, Akron, the Georgia Institute of Technology and the Air Force Research Laboratory.
The material invented by the team of scholars is an encirvasion of the existing ones that use a particular carbon structure, called carbon nanotubes.
“The principle of glue can be conceived as a series of pipes arranged like a pile of noodles,” explains Zhong Lin Wang of the Georgia Institute of Technology in Atlanta.
The scientists were inspired by gecko’s paws, coated with microscopic hairs that ensure a perfect adherence to the animal.
Prostems conducted on different materials, such as glass, Teflon and glazed paper, have shown that the new adhesive is ten times more powerful than the paws of these reptile reptiles and at the same time “immediately detaches itself from the wall,” explains Liming Dai, a researcher at the Faculty of Engineering at the University of Dayton.
WELMPING MURIS? - "This material may be useful as a transient sticker" adds Liming Dai.
Perhaps to walk on the walls just like Spider-Man? Maybe yes, but the researchers think of a much less fascinating use like the connection between electronic devices, because carbon nanotubes are conductors of heat and electricity.
Other applications could relate to aerospace engineering.
There's no super-heroes right now.
ALTRA RESEARCH – Even the University of Berkeley, Calif., is dedicated to finding a new sticker.
According to an article in the American Chemical Society magazine, the Californians presented an adhessive consisting of micro-fibers like hair that self-pull from dust during movement on a surface to always maintain a perfect adherence.
Pharmacies: never with fruit juices
Catitiff with grapefruit, orange and apple juice.
These beverages, consumed especially in the summer because quenchers and rich in vitamins, if taken at the same time as some life-saving drugs alter its absorption by amplifying or decreasing, depending on the case, the effectiveness with even serious consequences for health.
Reiter the dangers of both packaged and fresh fruit juices associated with the intake of some medicines David Bailey, a professor at the University of Western Ontario in London, who from the 236th national meeting of the American Chemical Society underway in Philadelphia launches a new alarm: just a glass of grapefruit, orange and apple juice to reduce the uptake of some life-saving drugs, nullifying its beneficial effects and jeopardizing the health of patients at risk.
Grapefruit juice had already come under fire because it was held responsible for increasing the effects some antiarrhythmic, anti-aximal, anxiolytic, anti-histamine and neurologic medicinal products considered to be toxic.
FARMACI A RICHIO - The same Italian Agency of the drug had announced it in an information bulletin two years ago alerting doctors and patients not to underestimate the danger deriving from this mix and to pay attention to what you drink in the event you take drugs such as Diazepam or methadone.
NO PLEATER ONLY - According to the experts, orange and apple juice also contain substances that inhiinfuse the drugs.
Along the list of active ingredients inhibited by grapefruit and other sucities.
The appeal of Michele Carruba, director of the pharmacology department of the State University of Milan: "These discoveries are usually immediately reported to the various international and national drug agencies that may change the information notes of the drugs.
“Whether we’re always reading the leaflets for the medicines you’re taking and if there’s anything you don’t know, don’t be impressed but ask for clarification from your doctor or pharmacist.”
The Economics Nobel to Paul Krugman
The Nobel Prize in Economics was awarded to the American Paul Krugman for his studies on trade models and the localization of economic activities.
Krugman, 55, is a neo-Keynesian who teaches at Princeton University and a well-known New York Times columnist.
“Krugman is one of the top critics of the Bush administration’s economic and foreign policy choices.
A few months after the attacks of September 2001, in the days when Enron failed, Krugman had foreseen in an op-edition published in the New York Times that the second event would be more decisive than the first, in terms of consequences on the history of the United States.
A prediction that sparked not a few controversies and that for years was reproached to the economist, but which has now turned out to be correct.
“I’m surprised and also a little shocked by the speed at which reminiscent of scandals such as the collapse of Enron or Worldcom have vanished from public attention,” Krugman said at the end of 2002.
According to Lorenzo Bini Smaghi of the Executive Committee of the European Central Bank, Krugman is an "economist of great value", but also a disseminator with a "very aggressive style, particularly critical of the excess of liberalism of the Bush administration".
Open Office, here is the third edition "anti-Microsoft"
OpenOffice.org 3.0, the open-source suite of personal productivity that aims to reach more and more users, from home to office, from professionals to public administration, as a free alternative to the most well-known Microsoft Office.
After almost a year-of-a-year work by volunteer developers, supported by SUN Microsystem, the new Open Office shows itself to the general public with several new features: at the welcome screen and the revised and rendered less spartan and rendered less spartan, are added other more substantial ones such as native compatibility for the Apple Mac OS X operating system and support for the new ODF (Open Document Format) 1.2, the latest ISO standard version.
Attention is also attached to interoperability with the Office 2007, Open Office is able to open the documents with the new Microsoft extension (.docx, .xlsx, .pptx).
Interesting some specific functions of some modules such as the one that allows you to reduce the size of PowerPoint presentations, useful for example to send them by e-mail, or the ability to import PDF files in the graphic processing module, preserving the original appearance of the page.
On this front you can create "Hybric PDF" documents, which connect the typical graphics of the Adobe format with the elements of a spreadsheet or a text processor.
The collaboration is an element that has received several improvements within the applications: in the spreadsheet you can intervene with multiple hands between different users, avoiding the conflicts on the changes, in the word processor, each collaborator is assigned a color in the comments for a better recognizability under review.
Some companies are also working to bring some OpenOffice capabilities to the web (on-the-cloud), and over the next few weeks they are expected to release the ultimate result.
The Italian version can be downloaded at http://it.openoffice.org/download/3.0.0/download300. html and has been in addition to the English one that has been on mirrors since Friday.
In addition to Windows and Mac OS, as usual the suite is also available for the Linux operating system.
On request the installation cd can also be dispatched at home or at the workplace.
And if the theme of gratuitousness can be a driving force for the diffusion in the houses, it is precisely in the business field that Open Office the suite tries the big bet, already being able to count on several projects carried out in the public administration and schools.
And there are also companies that offer an ideal training, consulting and assistance service for small and medium-sized companies, which could open up business prospects.
Open Office is made up of five main modules: Writer, for the corresponding videocrimination of Word; Calc, is the spreadsheet that aims to replace Excel; Impress, for Powerpoint emulation presentations; Dbase, as in the case of the most famous Access is the program that allows you to work on the databases; Draw, is finally the graphical module.
The objective for 2008 is to reach 5 million downloads, and considering that to date we are at almost 3 million and 600 thousand, with the release of the new version it is likely that we can also exceed the expected figure.
Kohl also tested positive is the fourth doping case.
New case of positivity at the last Tour de France: it is the Austrian Bernhard Kohl, the Gerolsteiner team, third classified and winner of the best climber's jersey.
To report the news is the newspaper L'Equipe website.
The 26-year--year-old Kohl is the fourth-spectaful runner at Cera, the third-generation EPO, after the Italians Riccardo Riccò and Leonardo Piepoli and the German Stefan Schumacher, also of Gerolsteiner.
Analyses on Kohl's blood samples are among those carried out in recent weeks by Lausanne and Chatenay-Malabry labs.
If the counter-analyses confirm the positivity to Cera, the Austrian will lose the third place at the Tour and will meet the two-year canonical disqualification.
Reconciling Kohl's positivity to the third-generation EPO, Pierre Bordry's head of the French anti-doping agency, Pierre Bordry, who told the Associated Press that he had notified the positivity to the Austrian authorities.
The 26-year-year-year-year-year-year-year-year-old was on the final podium behind the Spanish Carlos Sastre and American Cadel-Emplo Evans, who also won the polka dot jersey for the best climber.
IMMIGRATI:NEW YORK TIMES KEYS, ITALY IS RACIENT?
Italy "as an e-migratient country has only recently turned into a privileged destination for immigrants" reluctantly.
That’s turning the Belpaese into a nation of Racists? It’s what the New York Times enquires about a long article in a long article about racism in Italy starting from the case of the juvenile navor of Burkina Faso killed last month in Milan for stole a box of biscuits.
This case underscores what the NYT calls the "paradox of the north": the northern regions are at the same time those where "the most integration and the most job" but also the "highest anti-immigrant feeling in the country and the highest support to the Northern League".
Confirming the growing tension between Italians and immigrants the NYT has also cited the episodes of intolerance in Milan, Parma, Rome, Ciampino and Castel Volturno.
It's not just that.
Even the new legislation desired by the League that tightens the brakes on immigration and facilitates the expulsion of illegal immigrants is cited as a paradigm of climate change in Italy.
Tendency emphasized by both the Pope and the President of the Republic: "Giorgio Napolitano meeting Benedict XVI at the Quirinale has asked that the State and the Church work together to defeat racism".
Students victims of dear-laffiti up to 900 euros for a room
“Restaurants’ rental market knows no crisis.
According to Sunia’s latest survey, the tenants’ union, taking a single room can cost a student of up to 900 euros in Milan, €700 in Florence, 650 in Naples, €600 in Rome.
"The phenomenon has now taken on very serious characteristics to the point of excluding entire groups of young people from the right to study for the very high overall costs that the families of origin should support - explains the head of the Sunia study office, Laura Mariani - in these cities the strong demand from students has so deformed the market as to trigger a process of widespread increase even for residents".
In the majority of cases the rental offers then contain a series of violations: free-type contracts, unregistered, without the limits of canon, sub- rentals.
Extraordinary action is needed for the Sunia which, by revising taxation, can calm prices.
Looking at the details of the survey, we see that in Milan, for a bed in the Brianza area, it takes about 450 euros; for a single room we go from a minimum of 650 (Black Bands area) to an average value of 800 (Zone Lambiate, Udine and Fiera) at a maximum of 900 (in the Vittoria area).
In Florence a bed costs an average of 350/400 euros, a room about 700.
In Bologna, where the students are concentrated in the areas close to the University, it takes 250/280 euros for a double bed, from 370 to 500 for a single.
In Rome in areas close to the central universities (San Lorenzo, Piazza Bologna) are asked about 600 euros for a single room, 450 for a double bed.
Only slightly lower (550 euros for a single room) in areas close to the other Universities (Ostiense and Cinecittà).
It saves itself only if you move in peripheral areas: 300 euros for a bed and 450 a single in Prenestina, Centocelle and the like.
In Naples for a bed you need 300/450 euros, for a room you spend from 400 to 600 euros, with the highest prices in the Policlinico, Vomero and Colli Aminei areas.
Bari for a bed it takes 250/350 euros, at least 350 for a single.
In the smaller cities the prices are lower in absolute terms, but they have a greater weight in the city's economy.
In the cities where the phenomenon of rents to non-communicipal students is more frequent, such as in Perugia, a further anomaly occurs: the increase of about 25/30% of the fee requested from foreign students compared to that applied to Italians.
Farewell old cinema Paradiso, the cities erase the historic halls
The historical cinemas close in favor of Multiplex.
"A city with no movie theaters is a blind city, it's like an eyeless face."
The image offered by the director Giuseppe Tornatore effectively photographs what is happening in the large Italian urban centers but also in the cities of the province.
The process of marginalization of the cinemas, with the birth in the periphery of the great multisales and the consequent death by consumption of monosales in the central areas of the cities, is one of those events that appear inecapable and incontrovertibly but it also has all the characteristics of a real cultural crime.
To disappear there are often historical places full of remembrance, "It's as if a friend comes missing, an acquaintance with whom we have shared a piece of the street and a lot of emotions", observes the director of Nuovo cinema Paradiso, the film that twenty years ago earned Tornatore the Oscar and the Grand Jury Prize of Cannes, and which retraished the poetry of a provincial room and the nostalgia for a way of living cinema lost perhaps forever.
The figures provided by ANEC, the main association of exhibitors, are clear: in the last five years 316 traditional cinemas have closed the doors, and to pay the greatest price are above all the cinemas in the main cities: 10 less in Florence, 9 in Milan, Naples, Bologna and Rome, 6 less in Turin, 5 in Palermo.
Among the regions most affected by the closures, Emilia-Romagna excel with 82 single-wine-win-renowned cinema, followed by Tuscany (44) and Lombardy (41).
And to prove that the first responsible for their disappearance is the advent of multiplex, these are the three Italian regions that have registered the largest number of new multiplex cinemas: 195 more screens in Lombardy in the last five years, 81 both in Tuscany and in Emilia-Romagna.
"The Italian exercise has developed irrationally" observes Paolo Protti, president of the Anec "It is useful that new structures are built but it would be necessary to guarantee the survival of those already existing, because when a city hall closes, not all its visitors are willing to move into multiplex.
Not to mention the non-approval for the fund in favor of the merchants: the previous debt of the State is about 10 million euros.
“It is disgraceful, from an ethical and a moral point of view, that the Government does not respect existing laws.
To support the sector it would be needed between EUR 16 billion and EUR 18 million a year.
Interesting the case of Bologna, the first Italian city that, worried about the closures, has decided to run for cover with the signing of a protocol between Municipality and Anec, the first of its kind in Italy, to protect and promote mono-chauine cinema both in the historic center and in the suburbs.
Among the rules laid down there are tax-facilitation measures in favor of managers and advantages for the public in terms of mobility: viewers will be able to enter the restricted traffic area and will have special rates for parking and in some parking lots.
"It's an initiative that I like" observes Giuseppe Tornatore "and I hope that other cities will follow the example of Bologna by setting up other measures.
It is certain, however, that in order to counteract the overpowering of multiplex traditional rooms must also improve the quality of the sound and the image, because the audience today is more demanding.
“Tenuation of traditional cinema is a theme that for me has always been important to me: the closure of Bagheria Supercinema, the place of my childhood and adolescence, it was the spark that convinced me to make “New Cinema Paradise”, to which I’ve been thinking for 10 years.
“I have been living in Rome for 25 years, and when I pass in front of the Rialto or the Quirinale of Via Nazionale now closed, where I have experienced so many shared emotions, not to see the billboards blackened by the dust I turn on the other side”.
Ties Between Politics and 'ndrangheta Arrested mayor of Gioia Tauro
"The mayor of Gioia Tauro was willing to align the choices of the Municipality to the interests of cosca Piromalli".
There are heavy accusations that the magistrates of the Anti-Mafia Directorate of Calabria address the mayor of Gioia Tauro Giorgio Dal Torrione and Rosario Schiavone, and the mayor of Rosarno Carlo Martelli, arrested this morning for external concurrence in Mafia association.
Joacchino Piromalli in handcuffs, the elder mediator between the clan and the public administrations, and his nephew solicitor, homonymous of the boss, already convicted of association for mafia-style delinquency, condemned to compensate 10 million euros.
"In contemptuous of any juridical and moral norms, as well as of common sense", the prosecutors write, the two local administrations had expressed a willingness to pay advice to lawyer Piromalli.
"It would thus have been granted to the cosca - the judges - to officially enter the municipalities by facilitating the possibilities, already large, of control and direction of the public administration".
Agazio Loiero, President of the Calabria-Caulisian regional juvenile that "collusion between local authorities and organized malaviated there are a lot" in his region: "There are areas where Mafia intensity is stronger than in others, where collusion is almost unavoidable.
“But there are a lot of local politicians who resist the pressures of the ‘Ndrangheta, heroes who remain clean.”
The arrests of this morning follow those of last July with which the police announced to have beheaded the cosca Piromalli.
Since the end of last April, Gioia Tauro's city council was dismantled but already in 1991, and again due to alleged constraints between organized crime and the local administration, the local politicians had been relieved from the task of administering the Municipality.
Giorgio dal Torrione, mayor of the UDC at the time of the dissolution, had been elected head of a center-right coalition, after the run-off in May 2006.
He cried the scandal when the Prefecture ordered the dissolution of the city council.
Marco Minniti then Deputy Minister of the Inatrigue Marco Minniti denounced, accusing him of concocting everything for a political question, but the judges, in the arrest ordinance signed this morning, explain that Piromalli cosca had even arrived to ask for the variation of the A3 project.
The environmental interdeals ordered against Dal Torrione emerged "the readiness to espouse the needs of an entrepreneur, representative of a larger group notoriously close to the Piromalli, aligning the choices of the public administration with the interests of the cosca".
When the energy arises out of the waves now the big ones come down in the field
“It’s an strewn field, which occupies more than 70 percent of the world’s surface of the planet and is now unraveling the world’s global energy companies.
Waves, currents, tides are the new renewable sources on which they have put their eyes, and now they try to put their hands, giants like Chevron.
The possibility of obtaining energy from the movements of the sea has been studied for a hundred years and in the 70s, in conjunction with the first oil crisis, the research intensified.
When the price of the oil dropped, around 1980, the idea of leveraging marine energy was put aside, but now the environmentalist demands and the new increase in oil give impetus to the sector.
The World Energy Council (WEC) of London estimated in 2007 that 15 per cent of the world’s electricity needs could be covered by sloping offshore ocean facilities.
This amount would be twice that currently produced by the nuclear power plants.
In addition there would be currents and tides.
Colossi to the attack. - Yeah.
The first company to bet on marine energy was Enersis, which in 2005 signed an agreement to build a plant in the North of Portugal.
When the agreement was announced, it seemed like a revolutionary step.
Two-year-deceither two-year-deficit betting on marine energy is more and among these companies that have always been devoted to traditional energy.
“Chevron’s studying ocean energy technologies,” a spokeswoman for a subsidiary of the U.S.-oblical oil giant told the U.S.-Official-and-mono-Efono World Health Organization (Whether) and is considering cooperating with us.
The co-protection in question relates to obtaining permission from the U.S. Committee on Energy, which is necessary to launch a project to exploit marine energy in Alaska.
It’s not a small sign, especially when you’re considering that the U.S. in Alaska continues to extract oil and President Bush has recently launched a plan for new drilling.
In addition to the Chevron there are Verdant Power Inc. of New York and the Ocean Power of New Jersey to follow the path of energy that comes from the sea and the sums invested so far, although not known in the details, indicate according to the observatories an increasing interest.
Technology to be fine-tuning.
The experts speak of a technology still in its infancy, also because marine energy does not escape all the doubts about the environmental impact.
For now, plants such as Portugal or some in Scotland are small and do not seem to create major problems for the ecosystem.
To produce energy on a large-censiar scale, however, technologies with a greater environmental impact must be used.
And the choices are different: the Ocean Power, for example, uses a buoy system connected to each other.
The thrust of the waves, which moves buoys up and down is converted to hydraulic pressure by pistons and cylinders inside the floats.
The blood pressure spins a swirl which in turn feeds a generator.
Entities are sent ashore through submarine hoses.
For so far there are four plants of this type being designed, waiting only for federal approval and part of the energy could be used for a military installation.
The Verdant Power, on the other hand, already produces energy for a shopping mall and a parking lot using six underwater turbines in the East River of New York.
The movement of the water when tides circulates turbine blades, creating a rotary movement that feeds a generator.
La Verdant said that it has a long list of customers waiting for the necessary authorizations to take advantage of energy.
After the waves, the tides.
Particular interest, they say at the Institute for Electricity Inquiry, deserves the potential of the tides at this time.
Although it’s an inter-intermittent phenomenon, tide is more predictable than the wind, the sun or the waves, and therefore more reliable as an energy source.
It’s precisely on tidal energy that states such as New York, Maine and Alaska, along with others bordering the coast, have already invested US$7.5 million in 2008 and projected investment of up to $35 million in 2009.
